These steps install the server **in the current project**. The file goes in the project, and the
configuration goes in the `.mcp.json` of the project. Nothing is written outside the project
directory, and nothing changes for other projects on this machine. To install the server for every
project instead, read the last section.

## Prerequisite

```
dotnet --version
```

The output must start with `10.`. `dotnet run <file>.cs` with `#:package` directives is a
.NET 10 SDK feature, and every earlier SDK fails. If the version is lower, read
{{ site.url }}/install.md before you continue.

## 1. Put the file in the project

From the root directory of the project:

bash or zsh:

```
mkdir -p .mcp-servers
curl -fsSL -o .mcp-servers/{{ serverData.name }} "{{ site.url }}/servers/{{ serverData.id }}/{{ serverData.name }}"
```

PowerShell:

```
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force .mcp-servers | Out-Null
Invoke-WebRequest -Uri "{{ site.url }}/servers/{{ serverData.id }}/{{ serverData.name }}" -OutFile ".mcp-servers/{{ serverData.name }}"
```

The file starts with a `// ---` metadata comment block. It is valid C#, and `dotnet run`
ignores it.

## 2. Build one time

```
dotnet build .mcp-servers/{{ serverData.name }} -v q
```

The first build restores the packages and compiles, which takes from 30 seconds to two
minutes on a cold cache. An MCP client gives a server about 30 seconds to start, so a
server that was never built reports a failure that is only a timeout.

This command adds no `bin` directory and no `obj` directory to the project. A file-based app keeps
its build output in a cache of the user, under `%TEMP%/dotnet/runfile/` on Windows and
`$TMPDIR/dotnet/runfile/` elsewhere. The project directory holds one `.cs` file and nothing else, so
`.gitignore` needs no new line.
