## {% if serverData.envVars.length %}4{% else %}3{% endif %}. Register the server in this project

Claude Code:

```
claude mcp add {{ serverData.id }} --scope project{% if serverData.envVars.length %}{% for envVar in serverData.envVars %} --env {{ envVar | safe }}={{ "${" }}{{ envVar | safe }}{{ "}" }}{% endfor %}{% endif %} -- dotnet run ./.mcp-servers/{{ serverData.name }} -v q
```

`--scope project` writes `.mcp.json` in the root directory of the project. Commit that file, and
every person who clones the project gets the server. The `--` separator is required, because the
command that follows holds the `-v q` option.

A client that reads a JSON configuration file. Claude Code, Claude Desktop, Cursor, and
Windsurf use `mcpServers`. Put this in `.mcp.json` in the root directory of the project:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "{{ serverData.id }}": {
      "type": "stdio",
      "command": "dotnet",
      "args": ["run", "./.mcp-servers/{{ serverData.name }}", "-v", "q"]{% if serverData.envVars.length %},
      "env": {
{%- for envVar in serverData.envVars %}
        "{{ envVar | safe }}": "{{ "${" }}{{ envVar | safe }}{{ "}" }}"{% if not loop.last %},{% endif %}
{%- endfor %}
      }{% endif %}
    }
  }
}
```

Visual Studio and VS Code use `servers` in place of `mcpServers`. The object inside is the
same.

The path is relative to the project, so it stays correct for every person who clones the project, on
every operating system. It resolves against the working directory of the client, which is the project
directory. A client that starts a server somewhere else needs an absolute path instead.
{%- if serverData.envVars.length %}

Claude Code expands `${...}` from the environment when it reads `.mcp.json`, so the file holds the
name of the variable and no secret. A client that does not expand a variable needs the literal value,
and that configuration must then stay out of version control.
{%- endif %}

